WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:05.010
- This is the world's fastest gaming GPU,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.510
as of yesterday.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:11.040
Today, Nvidia launches this:

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.120
the GeForce RTX 4090,
a massive improvement

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.150
over its predecessor in almost every way.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.970
So, why aren't I more excited?

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.550
The fact that I could, you know,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.850
buy a cheap used car for
what Nvidia is asking for,

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:27.780
this aside,

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.470
there are some other problems
that we need to talk about,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.630
right after I tell you about our sponsor.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:34.860
- [Sponsor] Zoho CRM.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.530
Zoho CRM is a 360 degree solution

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.200
that offers an intuitive
UI, AI predictions,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.710
and a design studio to help
get your sales done faster.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:48.556
Get 50% off with Code ZCRM50
using the link down below.

00:00:48.556 --> 00:00:51.139
(upbeat music)

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.450
- Two years ago the
GeForce RTX 3090 launched

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:04.290
at an eye watering 1500 US dollars,

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.030
and Nvidia's justification for this was

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.580
that it was a Titan class GPU.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.910
Now of course, we all
know now that that was BS

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:16.320
as Nvidia launched the RTX
3090 Ti just six months ago,

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.230
in March of 2022, at two grand,

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.630
with a very modest spec
bump for the price,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.060
and still no Titan class

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.070
floating point compute functionality.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.210
Both cards are available at

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.190
around a thousand dollars
or so today thanks to

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.620
the crash in GPU demand and competition

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.020
from AMD is actually coming in
a little bit less than that.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:38.910
The RTX 4090 that launches at $1,600 US.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.290
What do you get for the price
of three PS5 gaming consoles?

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:47.130
It's not a Titan class GPU,
and it has the same amount

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.980
of VRAM as the previous gen RTX 3090,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.560
but, that's where the similarities end.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.450
Not only is the memory as fast

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.600
as what you'd find on an RTX 3090 Ti,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.180
you're getting over 50% more CUDA cores

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.810
that are each clocked at over 35% higher.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:05.310
I mean, say what you want,

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.130
but that alone would be
a substantial upgrade

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.530
for a mere 7% increase in MSRP,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:13.443
which is half the rate
of inflation since 2020.

00:02:13.443 --> 00:02:16.380
"And what a steal," is what I would say

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.420
if it weren't for the fact
that it's, it's honestly,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.630
it's still the price of an
entire mid-range gaming PC

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.800
on its own.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.440
So what else does this bring to the table?

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.780
Nvidia claims that each
of their CUDA cores

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.390
is beefed up compared to the
outgoing ampere architecture

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.580
in almost all respects,

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.160
with claimed performance
improvements of up to two times

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.900
what the 30 series could achieve.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.600
That's thanks in large part
to nearly double the L1 cache,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.180
and a substantial change
in the core layout itself.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.450
And also to the use of
TSMC's new N4 process

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.310
that allows the total
Die Area to be nearly

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.150
150 millimeters squared
smaller than its predecessor.

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:53.790
Of course, we need to
test Nvidia's claims,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.620
so we cut the lamps to fire up

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.620
our shiny new socket am5
bench with a fresh install

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.760
of Windows 11 22H1 and
go to town with testing.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.650
Why 22H1?

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.560
Apparently 22H2 introduced some issues

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.270
that Nvidia is not
going to have ironed out

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.070
by the time this video airs.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.350
But for now let's turn our attention

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.930
to the main event: Games.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.520
And right out of the gate,

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.520
even the traditional raster
performance is spectacular

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:23.130
compared to the mighty 3090 Ti.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:25.080
Yes, you're seeing that correctly.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:30.030
Cyberpunk 2077 ran 2.5
times faster on the 4090

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.330
than the 3090 Ti, bringing
minimum frame rates

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:36.780
up from sub 50 to over a hundred.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.830
We had to triple check that
just to be sure, but here it is.

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.900
We're also looking at minimum
frame rates beyond 120

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.950
at 4K in Forza Horizon
5, compared to sub 90

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.250
on the 3090 Ti, and it
is incredibly stable.

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.890
Assassins Creed, Valhalla's
frame rate also shot up

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:57.780
by about 50% on the 4090
enabling 4K 120 FPS gameplay

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.340
without any compromises.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.280
Remember, this is just the
rasterization performance,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.680
what we thought Nvidia was trying to hide

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:06.810
from us by showing off
their RT performance

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.400
in the press materials.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.470
Far Cry 6, another Ubisoft title,

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.960
doesn't see quite the
same improvement, however,

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.720
at about 30% or so across the board.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:19.200
It's not bad, like not at
all, but I'm just ruined

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.540
after seeing those other results.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.400
Improvements continue
to become more modest

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.240
in Shadow of the Tomb Raider,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:29.010
where we're likely starting
to get CPU limited at 4K.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.020
I'll let that sink in for a moment.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:32.753
Maybe wash it down with
a big swig from our

00:04:32.753 --> 00:04:35.580
chungus 64 oz water
bottle from lttstore.com.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.490
We see exactly that problem
again when we run CS:GO,

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:40.530
where, for some bizarre reason,

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.020
the situation is flipped on its head.

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.810
The RTX 3090 Ti outperforms the 4090

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.700
throughout multiple runs.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.770
It's especially surprising because

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:53.550
the GPU core clocks and
the load remained high.

00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:54.900
I mean, if you've got an explanation,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.790
I'd love to see your take in the comments.

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.950
Dropping the resolution down to 1440P,

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:01.890
it seems like we're becoming
even more CPU bound,

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:04.140
but critically, the minimum frame rates

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.990
in Forza Horizon 5 are massively improved,

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:09.300
making for a smoother overall experience.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:12.060
Interestingly, CS:GO actually manages

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.160
to return to normalcy at 1440P.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.660
So, I don't know,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.600
maybe we've got a driver
bug at 4K or something.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:22.495
Now, do you still remember
that Cyberpunk result?

00:05:22.495 --> 00:05:24.780
(laughing) Yeah. Okay.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:26.970
Okay, so check this out.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.930
We're going from 25 FPS
on average on the 3090 Ti

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:35.930
to a staggering 96 FPS on
the 4090 at 4K without DLSS.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.300
That's almost four times the performance.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.850
And when we turn ray
tracing on in the much older

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:47.190
Shadow of the Tomb Raider,
the 4090 is capable

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:51.360
of nearly doubling the
minimum FPS of the 3090 Ti.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:56.360
That brings this title from
mostly 60 FPS or more at 4K

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.880
to a buttery smooth 100 to
120 FPS, all without DLSS.

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.760
With DLSS, in performance mode,
Cyberpunk manages an average

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:13.760
of 144 FPS with 1% lows over 120 FPS.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.190
That's right, you can
get buttery smooth 4K 120

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.960
with ray tracing on this card
if you're okay with DLSS.

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.300
Shadow of the Tomb Raider
in DLSS quality mode

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:25.890
is just ridiculous.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:30.810
Where the 3090 Ti can't quite
reach 90 FPS in 5% lows.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.570
The 4090 is fast enough
to comfortably drive

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:34.585
144 hertz 4K monitor.

00:06:34.585 --> 00:06:39.585
Now, you might be wondering
about the new DLSS 3.0,

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:43.170
Nvidia's AI frame generation technology.

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.900
We're wondering too.

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:49.590
Unfortunately, all of our
cards, even the third party ones

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.900
that we're not allowed to show you today,

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:55.980
all of them crashed a
lot, like a lot a lot,

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.870
even across multiple benches.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.620
As a result, the labs weren't
able to properly test DLSS 3.0

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:05.280
and we'd like more ray
tracing results than we got.

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.630
We'll have all of that missing data

00:07:06.630 --> 00:07:08.550
in a follow up so stay tuned.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:12.180
But okay, what if you're not a big gamer?

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:14.550
Well, you're in luck because this thing

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:17.550
is also a productivity beast.

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:19.950
In Blender it gets over
double the samples per minute

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.740
in both the monster and junk shop scenes

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:26.010
and just under double in
the older classroom scene.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.930
That's a substantial time savings

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:31.020
and might be worth it on its
own if you're a 3D artist.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:33.630
Similarly, our 4K DaVinci Resolve export

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.820
finished nearly a minute faster,

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.770
a difference of roughly 25%,

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:41.370
Something that that'll
also add up over time,

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.450
especially for timelines

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:45.180
with a lot of rendered graphical effects.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.370
SPECviewperf meanwhile shows massive gains

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:49.620
across the board, with the
most substantial improvements

00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.040
in 3ds Max, Maya, Medical, and Solidworks

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:55.170
at nearly double the score.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.600
While Creo only saw modest
performance improvement

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.220
of around 10%.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.250
Still, this is like looking at
a completely different class

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:05.760
of GPU, not a typical
generational improvement.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.080
With all of these performance gains,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.660
Maybe Nvidia had to price these at $1,600

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:11.580
if they had any hope at all of selling

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.740
through all of their mining surplus 3090s.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:16.800
Whew. And that's not everything either.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.560
Another big part of productivity
that many companies,

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.500
Intel and Apple included,
are taking very seriously

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.000
is video encoding,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.260
and Nvidia has had to prove that they're

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.820
not sleeping on the job.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.670
Not only do you get the
same high quality encoder

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:31.950
that first debuted on the RTX 20-series

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:34.560
as we saw in our DaVinci resolve test,

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.910
but you get two of them, and
they're both capable of AV1.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:41.580
AV1 is the new codec
that's likely to take over

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.170
for live and on-demand streaming

00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:45.120
on sites like YouTube and Twitch.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.940
And while it can produce
significantly better video quality

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.380
at the same bit rates,

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.600
it's significantly more time consuming

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.610
and difficult to encode,

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.820
unless you have hardware dedicated for it.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:08:59.610
Intel Arc launched with
it as a headline feature,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.890
and it's a safe bet that
it'll become more important

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.300
as time goes on.

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:06.480
Unfortunately, we don't
have the time right now

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.130
to do a proper test of it for the review.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:10.320
So again, stay tuned for a future video

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:12.150
comparing AV1 encoders.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:13.530
What I can tell you today is that

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:17.580
it's nearly as fast as
Nvidia's existing H.264

00:09:17.580 --> 00:09:20.373
and H.265 encoders, which
is pretty impressive.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:23.610
Of course, all of these capabilities come

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:25.560
with some drawbacks,

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.290
the first of which being the power draw.

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:29.820
In order to reach the power target

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:31.230
that Nvidia set for it,

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.070
the RTX 4090 universally
requires an ATX 3.0 connector

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.890
and comes with adapters for it in the box.

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:40.410
As the specs indicated, our RTX 4090

00:09:40.410 --> 00:09:43.110
draws nearly as much
power under gaming load

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:46.380
as the RTX 3090 Ti,
though it did stay closer

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.560
to 425 watts than its rated 450.

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:53.640
This is a sharp contrast against
the RTX 3090 and 6950 XT,

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.180
which consistently both pulled
nearly a hundred watts less.

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.180
This does not bode well for
an eventual RTX 4090 Ti.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:04.710
At least the power targets
on the 4080 series cards

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.550
are lower, though we don't have
any of those to test today.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.610
When we hit the RTX 4090 with
a more demanding load via

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:15.330
MSI Kombustor, power consumption
skyrockets to the red line

00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.690
for both the RTX 4090 and the 3090 Ti.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.970
Though, curiously about
halfway through it kind of dips

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:23.580
down to about 440, which
is kind of strange.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:26.430
Meanwhile, the RTX 3090
once again doesn't cross

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:30.480
that 350 watt threshold and
neither did the 6950 XT.

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:32.280
With great power of course comes

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:33.900
great thermal output capacity

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.330
and the RTX 4090 is no exception.

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:38.580
With thermals while
gaming tracking roughly

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:40.410
in line with the power consumption.

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:43.230
Its massive three and a half
slot cooler keeps the hotspot

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.270
at the 80 degree mark while gaming,

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:49.290
placing it squarely between
the RTX 3090 and the 6950 XT

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:50.820
despite the power draw.

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.070
A testament to Nvidia's cooler design.

00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.200
Core clocks were obviously way higher

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.550
than any previous gen card,

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:58.560
and crucially they remain just as stable

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:01.980
throughout the run of
about 2.6 to 2.7 gigahertz.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:04.049
When we look again at our
combustor results though,

00:11:04.049 --> 00:11:06.660
the hotspot temperature
crosses the 80 degree threshold

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.580
along with the RTX 3090 Ti,

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.520
and we again see that slight
dip halfway into the run.

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:14.370
While the 3090 sits below 70 degrees.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.740
Core clocks end up significantly lower

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.716
at around 2.25 gigahertz for the 4090

00:11:19.716 --> 00:11:22.260
with the 3090 Ti throttling down to below

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:24.180
its less power hungry sibling.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.180
Our AMD card meanwhile sort
of did a heartbeat pattern

00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.710
of boosting and throttling

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:31.110
that could result in uneven performance.

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.750
It's worth noting that these
tests were performed inside

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:36.990
of a Corsair 5,000 D Airflow
with a 360 millimeter rad

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:40.470
up top and 3 120 millimeter
fans drawing air up front.

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.650
So it's not like we were
starving the cards for oxygen

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:45.990
or feeding them hot air from the CPU.

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:48.360
In fact, in spite of all
the airflow we gave them,

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:52.320
the RTX 4090 and 3090 Ti
both caused internal case

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:56.790
air temperatures to hit
highs of 39 to 40 degrees,

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:59.100
with significantly higher
minimum internal temperatures

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:02.010
in the RTX 3090, all at
an ambient temperature

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.830
of around 21 degrees.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:08.970
That means that if your case
can just barely handle a 3090,

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:11.520
you won't be able to manage a 4090.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:13.772
Sorry, small form factor aficionados,

00:12:13.772 --> 00:12:16.080
this card is not for you.

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:18.810
After all that, you might
look at the specs sheet

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:21.007
and have some lingering questions like

00:12:21.007 --> 00:12:24.150
"why isn't Nvidia supporting
PCI Express Gen 5?"

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.940
and, "where's DisplayPort 2.0?"

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.860
And the answer to those
questions, both of them,

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:29.790
whether you like it or not,

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:32.190
is that Nvidia doesn't think you need them

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:34.233
on a $1,600 graphics card.

00:12:35.494 --> 00:12:38.070
(sniffing) You smell that?

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:41.370
Yeah, that's the distinct scent of copium.

00:12:41.370 --> 00:12:44.310
While it's true that a GPU
running a whole 16 lanes

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:48.390
of PCI Express Gen 5 probably
won't be super useful today,

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:51.810
a GPU running eight lanes
of Gen 5 certainly would be,

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:55.020
especially for those who also
want lots of NVMe storage.

00:12:55.020 --> 00:12:57.120
A good bet for a card of its class.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.130
Remember, the faster the PCI Express link,

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.600
the fewer the lanes you need

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.550
for the same bandwidth requirements.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:04.350
Nvidia should know this.

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:06.270
As for DisplayPort 2.0,

00:13:06.270 --> 00:13:07.657
the official line is,

00:13:07.657 --> 00:13:11.430
"DisplayPort 1.0 already
supports 8K at 60 hertz,

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:15.030
and the consumer displays
won't need more for while,"

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.400
which might sound fair until you realize

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:21.523
that you are also limited
to 120 hertz at 4K,

00:13:21.523 --> 00:13:26.340
a performance level the RTX
4090 can exceed, as we've seen.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.350
The only way to get higher refresh rates

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:32.010
at DisplayPort 1.4 is to
use Chroma subsampling

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:35.820
which is, to put it mildly,
a suboptimal experience

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:37.410
on such premium hardware.

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:40.498
240 hertz 4K displays exist today

00:13:40.498 --> 00:13:44.190
and will have DisplayPort
2.0 support soon.

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:46.800
Arc already supports it, and
RDNA 3 has been confirmed

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:49.140
to support DisplayPort 2.0 since May.

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:52.410
Nvidia is clearly trying
to save a buck again

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:55.740
on a GPU that costs as
much as a Series X, a PS5,

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:58.140
a Switch and a Steam Deck, combined.

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:01.230
Or maybe these cards were
just ready and waiting

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:04.050
in a warehouse for
longer than we realized.

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:05.700
It's incredibly amusing to me

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.270
that the first GPU that
might actually be capable

00:14:09.270 --> 00:14:13.830
of running 8K gaming without
asterisks is being handled

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:17.040
in such a lackadaisical
fashion by Team Green.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.900
The days of generational performance

00:14:18.900 --> 00:14:21.690
for dollar gains are over, they say.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.750
Moore's law is dead, they say.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:27.660
And yet the competition
doesn't seem to think so.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.190
While it's true that nobody can touch

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.160
the 4090's mammoth performance right now,

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.910
as we saw with the RTX 3090,
that won't last forever.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.830
And that's where things get weird.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:42.180
As it exists right now,
while the RTX 4090 chugs

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:44.910
as much power as an RTX 3090 Ti,

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.300
it is a massive upgrade
over both that card

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:51.960
and the vanilla 3090 in
nearly every respect,

00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:54.600
well in excess of its price increase.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.660
For content creators,
this is a no brainer.

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.210
But I cannot in good
conscience recommend gamers

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:01.980
with more dollars than sense go out

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:04.200
and purchase a piece of
hardware that is incapable

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.170
of driving the also very
expensive displays that

00:15:07.170 --> 00:15:09.840
might actually be able
to take advantage of it,

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:13.440
especially when a less expensive
and less power hungry card,

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:17.613
perhaps even Nvidia's own RTX
4080s, can do the same thing.

